#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096291 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7578c48-cc9e-5170-8ffe-5ebee6580677" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Decimals in One Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "0a5391bf-06ef-5099-a835-0375ff96c342" :question/math-academy-id "e-1038" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n${0.5x + 3y + 4 = 0; 4x =-20y - 20$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the first equation by $10$, as follows:\n\n$$\n{0.5x + 3y + 4 = 0 \\mid \\mid \\cdot 10; 4x =-20y - 20\n$$\n\nWe also notice that each equation can be simplified by dividing by a common factor. In the first equation, the common factor is $5$, while in the second equation, the common factor is $4$.\n\n$$\n{5x + 30y + 40 = 0 \\mid \\mid \\div 5; 4x =-20y - 20 \\mid \\mid \\div 4\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is already solved for $x$. So, we can substitute $x = -5y-5$ into the first equation and then solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 6y + 8 &= 0 \\\\\n(-5y - 5) + 6y + 8 &= 0 \\\\\ny + 3 &= 0 \\\\\ny &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $x$, we substitute $y=-3$ back into $x=-5y-5$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= -5y - 5 \\\\\n&=-5(-3) - 5 \\\\\n&= 15 - 5 \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x = 10$ and $y = -3$, which can be expressed as $(10, -3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096292 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc62d8df-450f-5f7a-b647-b53c051f8339" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Decimals in Both Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "8f4b321d-ebc7-5f78-9bde-b51b297a0cc0" :question/math-academy-id "e-1039" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n${1.4x + 2.8y = 1.4; 0.02x + 0.01y = 0.05$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the first equation by $10$ and the second equation by $100$, as follows:\n\n$$\n{1.4x + 2.8y = 1.4 \\mid \\mid \\cdot 10; 0.02x + 0.01y = 0.05 \\mid \\mid \\cdot 100\n$$\n\nTo simplify the first equation, we divide by $14$:\n\n$$\n{14x + 28y = 14 \\mid \\mid \\div 14; 2x + y = 5\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Notice that the first equation can easily be solved for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 2y &= 1 \\\\\nx &= 1 - 2y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can substitute $x=1-2y$ into the second equation and then solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + y &= 5 \\\\\n2(1 - 2y) + y &= 5 \\\\\n2 - 4y + y &= 5 \\\\\n2 - 3y &= 5 \\\\\n- 3y &= 3 \\\\\ny &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $x$, we substitute $y=-1$ back into $x=1-2y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 1 - 2y \\\\\n&= 1 - 2(-1) \\\\\n&= 1 + 2 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x =3$ and $y =-1$, which can be expressed as $(3, -1)$."}]}
